1. What This Screen Is
1.1 Primary Function and Design Philosophy
The Welcome Window is the first screen that appears when you launch AquaStream360, a comprehensive water system analysis platform. From this single screen, users can access every major application and tool needed for network modeling, weather analysis, testing, calibration, water quality assessment, data visualization, and overall evaluation of water systems. This screen is designed to allow users to quickly access key features without launching the full application, providing a faster and more convenient workflow.

1.2 Interface Structure and User Workflow Zones
The window is organized into three vertical panels aligned with user intent: configuration, core operations, and specialized tool access.
•	Left Panel: User Configuration and Session Management
o	Purpose: Capture and persist user-specific information to tailor workflows, report generation, and module behavior across sessions.
o	Components: Input fields for Country, City, Name, Profession, and Organization, accompanied by Save and Clear buttons. This ensures consistent personalization across all modules and submodules.
•	Center Panel: Core Platform Portal
o	Purpose: Provide direct access to the entire integrated AquaStream360 platform, encompassing advanced modeling, hydraulic and water quality analysis, GIS-based spatial analysis, and network simulation.
o	Components:
	A prominent launch area for the full AquaStream360 suite, reflecting the massive scale and integrated nature of its core functionality.
	An overview panel describing platform capabilities, including multi-scale modeling, GIS mapping, scenario simulation, and data-driven decision support. This sets expectations for users about the platform’s breadth and power.
•	Right Panel: Modular Tool Access Matrix
o	Purpose: Offer single-click access to hundreds of specialized modules and utilities that extend the platform, including analytical, visualization, integration, and automation tools.
o	Components: Categorized grid of supplementary-applications such as Net Calibrator, Data Analysis, 3D GeoViewer, Data Visualizer, Python Automation, Weather and Topography Integration, and Feedback. Each module operates as a robust subsystem capable of supporting complex workflows independently of the main launch.
1.3 Ancillary Interface Elements
•	Dynamic Header Banner: Horizontally scrolling display highlighting the platform’s core capabilities, latest updates, and strategic value proposition.
•	Status Bar: Persistent footer showing system status, contextual guidance, and quick-reference links for ongoing tasks.

