2. Complete Application Guide
1. Water Distribution Engineering (Core Application Name)
This core application represents a significant advancement in software designed for the modeling, analysis, and evaluation of water distribution networks. As a comprehensive platform, it integrates a wide array of functionalities to support engineers, planners, and operators in enhancing decision-making quality, analytical accuracy, operational efficiency, and contextual relevance throughout their workflow. The software’s design philosophy emphasizes automation and intuitive interaction, allowing users to focus on interpretation and strategic decisions rather than on complex tool configuration.
Core Design and User Interaction
AquaStream360 is built around a self-guided, interactive model that communicates with users primarily through visual cues and optional audio feedback. The interface employs extensive color-based indicators to highlight required actions, incomplete steps, and areas of relevance based on the user’s current objectives. Features are enabled by default, and interactions typically result in immediate action execution or clear guidance on prerequisite steps. This design significantly reduces the learning curve and operational overhead.
Network-Driven and Geospatially Enabled Workflow
All functionality within AquaStream360 is contingent upon a network model. The software automatically guides users to generate or import a network if none is present, ensuring a structured workflow. Simulation is the next critical step, with the system prompting users to run simulations to unlock the full suite of analytical and visualization features. Georeferencing is fundamental to advanced capabilities, and AquaStream360 simplifies this process dramatically. Users can georeference a network in seconds using coordinates of two known points or by interactive map selection, without requiring GIS expertise or external software. This spatial enablement underpins the platform’s advanced GIS and mapping functionalities.
Modular Application Structure
The software is organized into three tightly integrated main subsections: Modeling, Analysis, and Monitoring.
a)	Modeling Section: Serving as the central hub, this section is far more than a model builder. It provides a comprehensive set of tools for network construction, modification, and evaluation. Key functionalities include:
o	Network Construction & Import: Automated tools like the Network Builder can generate calibrated hydraulic models from diverse data sources (Excel, CSV, Shapefiles, CAD, databases). Manual drawing and tracing from images are also supported.
o	Attribute & Operational Management: Users can define material libraries, perform bulk attribute edits, create time-based patterns, and set operational controls, curves, and performance thresholds.
o	Advanced Calibration & Resilience Tools: Modules like the Net Calibrator automate model calibration against field data. The Resilience and Failure Analysis tools, including the detailed Pipe Failure Test popup, allow users to assess system robustness and component integrity.
o	Zoning: Tools for creating and analyzing network zones (e.g., District Metered Areas) for pressure management and leakage control.
o	Comprehensive Visualization: The section provides direct access to 2D/3D flow animations, pressure maps, and a vast array of graphical analysis plots.
o	Results Management: A dedicated Simulation Results Page allows for filtering, statistical analysis, and export of hydraulic and quality results.
b)	Analysis Section: This section focuses on advanced, systematic investigation and bulk modification of network elements. It is organized into specialized pages:
o	Attribute Management: A powerful interface for viewing and applying bulk modifications to network elements based on conditions, lists, or topological features (e.g., critical bridges or articulation points).
o	Performance Investigator: A sandbox environment for risk-free "what-if" scenario testing. Users can modify single or multiple elements, add new components like leaks or fire demands, and instantly see the impact on key performance indicators without altering the original network.
o	Geospatial Analysis: A fully integrated GIS environment for building, editing, and analyzing networks directly on interactive maps. It includes tools for heatmaps, flow/pressure animations, buffer creation, and zone-based analysis.
o	Disaster Analysis: Enables modeling of extreme events (pipe breaks, earthquakes, contamination, power outages) to assess system vulnerability and resilience under emergency conditions.
o	Criticality & Resilience Analysis: Dedicated modules for identifying components whose failure has the greatest impact (Criticality) and for quantifying the system's overall ability to withstand and recover from disruptions using topological, hydraulic, and economic metrics.
o	Changes Investigator: A comparison dashboard that automatically highlights differences between network states (e.g., before and after a modification), quantifying impacts on performance and affected population.
c)	Monitoring Section: Designed for real-time and historical performance tracking, this module provides advanced monitoring capabilities and is under active development to expand its analytical and visualization features.
