12. Additional Notes & Platform Insights
AquaStream360 is designed to help engineers, planners, and decision-makers address complex challenges in water distribution systems, including aging infrastructure, operational inefficiencies, non-revenue water, and climate variability. The platform integrates a wide range of tools to support sustainable planning, design, and operations, enabling evidence-based decision-making across the full lifecycle of water management. It is updated frequently, with monthly releases delivering new features, refinements, and workflow improvements.
Automation and Scalability
One of AquaStream360’s key strengths is its ability to automate and scale modeling processes. Large, complex hydraulic models — including city-wide or multi-network systems — can be constructed in seconds when input data is properly formatted. Automation reduces manual drawing and data entry, enabling integration of multiple GIS formats, spreadsheets, and other spatial data sources with minimal preprocessing. Elevation values for network nodes can be automatically assigned from DEM (Digital Elevation Model) data, streamlining the setup of gravity-fed networks.
Persistent Network Context
Example networks are provided to help users learn, experiment, or use as templates. Once a network is generated or imported in any module, it persists across the application: all other modules automatically use the same network context without requiring re-import, unless the user closes the session or selects a different network. This ensures seamless interoperability between modeling, analysis, monitoring, and visualization modules.
Self-Guidance and Intuitive Interaction
A core design principle is self-guidance and visual feedback. All buttons and features use descriptive labels, and modules rely heavily on status tips, pop-up notifications, tooltips, and red highlighting to communicate required actions or missing inputs. Buttons are enabled by default to encourage exploration. If a required action is incomplete, the software responds proactively:
•	Clicking an analysis feature before generating a network highlights the entire input section in red for three seconds.
•	Attempting to analyze results before running a simulation highlights the simulation button in red.
•	If the section to be highlighted is not visible, a pop-up explains the missing action and prompts the user to generate a network or run a simulation before proceeding.
This approach helps users understand dependencies between actions, reduces errors, and maintains flexibility in workflow.
Comprehensive Reporting
AquaStream360 generates automated reports that may extend to hundreds of pages, covering hydraulic performance, resilience and criticality metrics, topographical analysis, cost evaluations, and more. Reports combine tables, charts, and visual maps and can be exported in multiple formats, including PDF, HTML, GIS files, CAD drawings, and EPANET models.
Water Quality and Weather Integration
The platform supports water quality and contamination tracing, simulating contaminant movement, persistence times, and propagation across the network. Advanced weather integration links historical and real-time climatic data (e.g., NASA POWER) with network performance and demand trends, enabling climate-informed operational planning and forecasting.
Monitoring Module
Still under development, the Monitoring module will provide real-time network performance tracking, anomaly detection (such as leaks), and dynamic simulation capabilities.
Undocumented Modules with Reliable Guidance
Overall, AquaStream360 includes dozens of additional modules that are not fully documented in this guide. However, these modules are supported by robust in-application guidance systems, making them easy to use. Many are advanced enough to function as independent applications. All modules are accessible from the main application, and once a network is generated in the core Water Distribution Engineering module, every other module automatically recognizes and uses it. If no network is present, modules prompt users to import one. These additional modules provide specialized functionality, including advanced hydraulic simulations, scenario testing, detailed resilience analysis, multi-dimensional 3D terrain evaluation, water quality management, and custom automation via Python scripting.

